    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "74ef74d2_fedf38cf",
        "filename": "services/proguard.flags",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1064003
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-04T20:08:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A good number do call private methods and even reference private fields. I went through the list and pruned out a few that aren\u0027t strictly necessary (I first went through these a few months ago, probably should have documented each addition \u003d/). However, others will require some more targeted refinement.\n\nOne issue is that we use manual JNI registration in native code to wire up each native method declared in Java. However, the `native \u003cmethods\u003e` keepclasseswithmembernames rule above doesn\u0027t actually seem to keep native methods that are *unreferenced in Java*, so some of them will get stripped. This was a little surprising at first, though the \"process native methods\" section @ https://www.guardsquare.com/manual/configuration/examples#androidapplication seems to support this. For some of these classes, that could suggest a bug, but for others it just means they have a few native-\u003ejava-\u003enative hops for certain methods.",
      "parentUuid": "51898af5_e0b35466",
      "revId": "748375b074e60e7e8f3be6c9081f502350e20b0f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
